<section><exercise id="_1ed3a45d-fc40-408f-8981-6afb3ff574a4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-36x\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! -18</para><para>The correct answer is -18.</para></solution></exercise></section><para>Some quadratic expressions have no linear terms. For questions 6 – 7, find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph representing each equation:</para>
<para>\(y=x^2-25\)</para>
<para>\(y=x^2+16\)</para>
<para>(Note it is possible for the graph to not intersect the \(x\)-axis.) If you get stuck, try graphing the equations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\).</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)?</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\((5,0)\),\((-5,0)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<section><exercise id="_9038b5dc-8f68-4b47-89af-ac89342ac1a8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 0</para><para>The correct answer is 0.</para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+16\).</item>
</list>
<exercise>
